THE SURFACE THE DEPTH GUARD COULD NOT SEE

verify-navigation passed at the baseline: 47 public routes, every one within
three clicks. It reads Header.tsx and SiteFooter.tsx and both were honest. The
third navigation surface — the command palette — it does not read, and that one
had been wrong since the site stopped being one page.

CommandPalette.tsx carried its own hand-written navigation:

    const go = (hash) => () => {
      close();
      document.getElementById(hash)?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' });
      history.replaceState(null, '', `#${hash}`);
    };

Six ids — services, process, reviews, faq, quote, gallery. Every one of them is
rendered by home-client.tsx and by nothing else. So on 46 of 47 public routes
the optional chain swallowed a null, nothing scrolled, the dialog closed, and
replaceState ran anyway and rewrote the address bar to a fragment matching
nothing on the page. Six of thirteen actions, silently dead, everywhere but the
homepage. A control that does nothing is worse than a missing control: the
visitor concludes the mechanism is broken and stops reaching for it.

Beyond those six it knew /design, and that was the whole of it. Not Floor
Studio, /pricing, /service-areas, /corridors, /estimate, /guides, /papers,
/case-studies, /quote-check or /framework — the pages somebody is most likely
hunting at the moment they press ⌘K.

AND TWO PAGES LIVED ONLY IN THE FOOTER

/pricing — the three published bands, the source every other surface on this
site links to when it names a number — appeared in no menu at all, desktop or
mobile. A price page reachable only by scrolling to the bottom is a price page
the visitor concludes does not exist.

/where-we-work — every published job, each linked to what was measured — the
same. It is not a duplicate of anything: /service-areas is where we will go,
/corridors is how we get there, /where-we-work is where we have been. The one
carrying the evidence was the one no menu named.

ONE SOURCE, THREE PROJECTIONS

The menus move to apps/web/lib/navigation.ts — no 'use client', no React — and
the surfaces project it: the desktop panels, the mobile drawer accordions, and
a palette whose destination list is DERIVED rather than written. A page added
to a menu is searchable the same commit and cannot be forgotten, because nobody
has to remember. This is the rule the rest of the repository already lives by,
applied to the one fact still kept in three places.

go() now takes an href. A fragment on the current page still scrolls — the good
behaviour it had on the homepage, kept — and anything else navigates.

An empty palette is a menu; a typed one is a search. Fifty-five rows at rest is
a sitemap with a text box, and it throws away what this component was
opinionated about: the first rows are the three questions that precede a
booking. So the resting state is the assistant, six high-intent destinations
and the phone, and the first keystroke opens the whole corpus. The shortlist is
selected BY HREF out of the derived set, so it carries the menus' own labels
and cannot describe a page differently from the panel that links it.

"Get a free estimate" now points at /estimate rather than /#quote. It worked,
and it cost a visitor deep in /service-areas/buffalo a round trip through the
homepage to reach a form that has its own page — the page Floor Studio already
hands a finished design to.

ONE WIDENING EDIT, DISCLOSED

scripts/verify-navigation.mjs CHROME gains apps/web/lib/navigation.ts. The
guard reads strings and the strings moved; without it the chrome would appear
to shrink by fifty links and pages a visitor reaches in one click would be
reported unreachable. It widens what counts as chrome. It loosens no rule,
lowers no threshold, exempts nothing, and MAX_DEPTH is untouched. Chrome link
count 43 → 44.

AND THE AUDIT-01 HARNESS NOW RUNS

scripts/audit ran under `node --experimental-strip-types`, which needs Node
≥ 22.6 and did not run on the machine it was written for. Evidence nobody can
reproduce is not evidence. It is a vitest characterisation test now
(lib/floor-studio/audit.test.ts) that pins AV-01 and AV-02 as numbers, so the
day VIS-02 or VIS-03 fixes one, the suite fails and the audit citing it has to
be rewritten in the same patch. A defect that can be fixed without anyone
noticing the audit went stale is a defect that comes back.

Verified: 66 guards pass. lib/navigation.test.ts and lib/floor-studio/audit.
test.ts execute green (216 assertions). 55 destinations, no duplicate href, no
label pointing at two pages, no bare fragments, every one of the twelve
previously unreachable pages present. Orphans among public routes: 3, all
correct — /design/spec (in-flow from the configurator), /r (short link),
/verify-email (email flow).

